{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Tripoli and was born around 1140 Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was around 12 years old During his reign he faced numerous challenges including conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Zengid dynasty He also had to deal with the internal struggles of the Crusader states Raymond III was married twice first to Eschiva of Saint Omer and then to Eschiva of Bures They had several children including Bohemond III who succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Second Crusade He joined the forces of King Louis VII of France and King Conrad III of Germany in their march to Jerusalem in 1148 Raymond III was one of the leaders of the Crusader forces at the Battle of Ager Sanguinis in 1154 where the Crusaders were defeated by the Zengid army He was also present at the Siege of Damascus in 1148 which was unsuccessful\n\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Byzantine Empire He initially allied himself with the Byzantines in 1158 but later became their enemy when he supported the rebellion of Prince Reynald of Châtillon against the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus Raymond III was captured by the Byzantines in 1165 and was held prisoner until 1176\n\nDespite these challenges Raymond III was able to maintain the County of Tripoli as a significant Crusader state He had a strong military and built several castles and fortifications to protect the county from external threats He also established good relations with the Assassins, a Muslim sect that controlled several mountain strongholds in the region Raymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader forces were defeated by Saladin's army\n\n*Family and early life*\n-----------------------\n\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Tripoli He was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was around 12 years old Raymond III was married twice first to Eschiva of Saint Omer and then to Eschiva of Bures They had several children including Bohemond III who succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli\n\n*Role in the Second Crusade*\n---------------------------\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Second Crusade He joined the forces of King Louis VII of France and King Conrad III of Germany in their march to Jerusalem in 1148 Raymond III was one of the leaders of the Crusader forces at the Battle of Ager Sanguinis in 1154 where the Crusaders were defeated by the Zengid army He was also present at the Siege of Damascus in 1148 which was unsuccessful\n\n*Conflicts with the Byzantine Empire*\n------------------------------------------\n\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Byzantine Empire He initially allied himself with the Byzantines in 1158 but later became their enemy when he supported the rebellion of Prince Reynald of Châtillon against the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus Raymond III was captured by the Byzantines in 1165 and was held prisoner until 1176 Despite these challenges Raymond III was able to maintain the County of Tripoli as a significant Crusader state He had a strong military and built several castles and fortifications to protect the county from external threats He also established good relations with the Assassins, a Muslim sect that controlled several mountain strongholds in the region Raymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader forces were defeated by Saladin's army.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.00045372682507149875}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.00016473367577418685}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.3620835542678833}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -1.0490362910786644e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logp